---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro'
import ActionBox from '../../components/ActionBox.astro'
import StatusRow from '../../components/StatusRow.astro'
import ServiceEntitlement from '../../components/ServiceEntitlement.astro'
import ServiceShot from '../../components/ServiceShot.astro'
import FlowDiagram from '../../components/diagrams/FlowDiagram.astro'

// Every screenshot below is captured by scripts/capture-services.ts,
// which performs the act and asserts the surface before capturing; the
// date travels with each shot. Re-run the script to refresh the evidence.
const captured = '2026-08-29'
---

<Base
  title="The Vocabulary"
  description="The OIML vocabularies as linked, diagrammed, addressable concepts: the VIM and VIML edition series, the curated Complete Vocabulary, and the G 18 term-usage registry. Public, no account."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Services"
    title="The Vocabulary"
    lede="Every term of legal metrology, linked: the VIM and VIML edition series, the curated OIML Complete Vocabulary, and the G 18 registry of how the publications actually use each term. Every concept has its own URL, so a citation can point at the term itself."
  />

  <section class="px-8 pb-20 max-w-5xl">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <ActionBox
        href="https://www.oimlsmart.org/vocab/"
        label="Open the vocabulary service"
        description="Public, no account. Search, browse the datasets, or open the graph view."
      />
      <ServiceShot
        service="vocab"
        id="vocab-home"
        alt="The vocabulary service home: eleven datasets, the VIML and VIM edition series with current editions marked, the curated Complete Vocabulary collection"
        caption="The eleven datasets: edition series, current editions marked, the curated collection"
        date={captured}
      />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">What you can do</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-8">
        Six acts, each performed against the live service before this page listed it; the dated capture is the audit artifact, re-produced by <span class="font-mono text-sm">scripts/capture-services.ts</span> on every refresh.
      </p>
      <ol class="grid gap-10 list-none pl-0">
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">1. Browse the whole terminology estate.</strong> Eleven datasets: the VIML and VIM edition series (every edition, the current one marked), the curated Complete Vocabulary collection across both, and the counts on the face: 7,451 concepts, five languages.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="vocab"
            id="vocab-home"
            alt="The dataset list: VIML four editions, VIM six editions, the Complete Vocabulary with 6,031 concepts"
            caption="Every edition, the current one marked"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">2. Search every edition at once.</strong> One query returns the term wherever it lives: <em>calibration</em> surfaces in the VIML 2022, each VIM edition back to 1984, and the curated collection, each hit with its concept number.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="vocab"
            id="vocab-search"
            alt="Search results for calibration, grouped per dataset: VIML 2022, VIM editions 2012 through 1984, and the Complete Vocabulary"
            caption="One query, every edition, each hit numbered"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">3. Open a concept and cite the term itself.</strong> <em>Calibration hierarchy</em> (VIM 2.40) opens with its definition, its partitive relations, its edition series, and its canonical URI. Citing a term means copying its URL; the citation points at the concept, not at a page range in a PDF.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="vocab"
            id="vocab-concept"
            alt="The concept page for calibration hierarchy: the definition, related concepts, the edition series with the current edition marked, languages, and the canonical URI"
            caption="One concept, one URL, the relations drawn"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">4. Read a term across languages.</strong> The concept page carries its languages (English and French on the capture above; the datasets span five), so a translator works from the governed term, not a paraphrase.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">5. See the relations drawn.</strong> The graph view renders the term relations as a navigable graph: the structure between concepts, not just the alphabet of them.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="vocab"
            id="vocab-graph"
            alt="The graph view: the vocabulary's term relations rendered as a navigable graph"
            caption="The graph view: the relations, drawn"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">6. Anchor your own artifacts to the governed concept.</strong> The platform's models and forms reference vocabulary terms by their concept URLs, and yours can too; the dataset detail, edition by edition with concept counts, lives on this site at <a href="/vocabularies/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">Vocabularies</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">How it works</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-4">
        The vocabularies are published as datasets, not documents: each edition is a structured concept set, the curated collection collates across the series, and the G 18 registry collates where each term is actually used across the Recommendations and checks each usage against the vocabulary definition.
      </p>
      <FlowDiagram
        caption="FIG. TERM TO CITATION"
        steps={[
          { label: 'The vocabularies', sub: 'VIM and VIML, every edition', accent: 'brand' },
          { label: 'The collection', sub: 'the curated Complete Vocabulary', accent: 'teal' },
          { label: 'The concept', sub: 'one URL, its languages, its relations', accent: 'amber' },
          { label: 'The citation', sub: 'models and forms anchor to it', accent: 'slate' },
        ]}
        arrows={[
          { label: 'collates', detail: '6,031 curated concepts' },
          { label: 'addresses', detail: 'a URI per concept' },
          { label: 'governs', detail: 'the term, not a copied definition' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">SMART today, SMART+ next</h2>
      <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6 mb-4">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.12em] text-ink-muted mb-2">SMART · LIVE TODAY</p>
        <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The datasets, the search, the concept URLs, the graph, the G 18 usage registry: all live, all performed above.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-dashed border-rule rounded-md p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.12em] text-ink-muted mb-2">SMART+ · THE ROADMAP TIER</p>
        <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The twin's served registers and the dataspace's shared vocabularies name governed concepts the same way, so the term a certificate cites is the term a twin serves: that anchoring is <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">roadmap</strong>, carried on <a href="/technologies/dataspace-interop" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">the dataspace and interop page</a>. Today the vocabulary serves the publications and the models, and this page claims only that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Status</h2>
      <StatusRow rows={['vocab-surface']} />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Who can use it, who can run it</h2>
      <ServiceEntitlement service="vocab" />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">The honest questions</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4">
        <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.125rem] mb-2">Why not just cite the PDF?</h3>
          <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">A PDF citation points at a document and a page; a concept URL points at the term. When a Recommendation, a model, or a form anchors to the concept, every reader resolves the same definition, in their language, with its relations.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.125rem] mb-2">Which edition is current?</h3>
          <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">The series marks it (VIML 2022, VIM 2012 today), and the older editions stay browsable: a 1993 citation still resolves to the 1993 concept, so historical documents remain checkable against the vocabulary they were written in.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.125rem] mb-2">Do I need an account?</h3>
          <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">No. The vocabularies are public artifacts of the OIML; the service asks for nothing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Where to go next</h2>
      <ActionBox
        href="https://www.oimlsmart.org/vocab/"
        label="Browse the vocabularies"
        description="Search is the fastest way in; the graph view is the most surprising."
      />
      <ActionBox
        href="/vocabularies/"
        label="Read the dataset detail"
        description="Edition by edition, with concept counts: the vocabularies as published datasets."
      />
      <ActionBox
        href="mailto:info@oimlsmart.org"
        label="Talk to us"
        description="info@oimlsmart.org, the programme's front-door address. Terminology and translation questions land here."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-rule pt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Verified {captured}: the service answered live and every act above was performed headlessly (the dataset browse, the cross-edition search, the concept page with its URI and languages, the graph view). The captures and their assertions are in public/img/services/manifest.json.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
